---

```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.path = "knox-",
                      fig.width = 7, fig.height = 6.5, dpi = 96, out.width = "100%",
                      message = FALSE, warning = FALSE)
```

Dengue is transmitted by *Aedes aegypti*, which flies short distances, and
the virus takes days (the extrinsic and intrinsic incubation periods) to
pass from one person to the next. So, when there is local transmission,
cases are **closer in space and time** than expected if space and time were
independent. The **Knox test** (Knox 1964) counts the pairs of cases that
are close in both space (less than `ds` metres) and time (less than `dt`
days) and compares that number with its distribution when the dates are
permuted at random (Monte Carlo). Each close pair is a **space-time link**,
and the linked cases form the **transmission chains**.

The example uses `sim_dengue`, **simulated** geocoded cases of the
metropolitan area of Guadalajara; with the real geocoded dataset the steps
are the same.

```{r setup}
library(denhotspots)
library(ggplot2)
```

## 1. The cases of a season

`knox_test()` needs a table with the coordinates (`x`, `y`) and the date of
onset of symptoms (`onset`):

```{r cases}
x <- subset(sim_dengue, ANO == 2024)
x <- data.frame(x = x$long, y = x$lat, onset = x$FEC_INI_SIGNOS_SINT)
nrow(x)
```

## 2. Knox test

The windows are 400 m (the flight range of *Ae. aegypti* plus the movement of
people around the house) and 20 days (the time between two cases of the same
chain); the coordinates are projected (`crs = "+proj=eqc"`, metres) and the
test uses 1,000 permutations.

```{r knox}
res <- knox_test(x = x, crs = "+proj=eqc", dt = 20, ds = 400, sym = 1000,
                 sp_link = FALSE, planar_coord = FALSE)
res$knox
```

- `knox`: number of pairs of cases close in space and time (space-time
  links);
- `p_value`: proportion of the permutations with at least as many pairs;
- `RR`: ratio between the observed and the expected number of pairs.

A small `p_value` (and `RR` > 1) means space-time interaction: local
transmission.

## 3. Map of the transmission chains

`res$space_time_link` has one line per link (EPSG:4326) and `res$x` the cases
(in the projected coordinates). The map shows the links coloured by the
epidemiological week of the cases, as `transmission_chains_map()` does in an
interactive map.

```{r chains, fig.cap = "Space-time links (400 m, 20 days) of the cases of 2024."}
cases <- sf::st_as_sf(res$x, coords = c("x", "y"), crs = "+proj=eqc")
cases <- sf::st_transform(cases, 4326)
cases$week <- cut(lubridate::epiweek(cases$onset), c(0, 25, 30, 35, 40, 45, 53),
                  labels = c("1-25", "26-30", "31-35", "36-40", "41-45", "46-53"))
linked <- lengths(sf::st_intersects(cases, sf::st_buffer(res$space_time_link, 1))) > 0

loc <- rgeomex::extract_locality(cve_edo = "14",
                                 locality = c("Guadalajara", "Zapopan", "Tlaquepaque", "Tonalá"))
pal <- c("#2166AC", "#1B9E77", "#A6D854", "#F2B134", "#E6550D", "#B2182B")

ggplot() +
    geom_sf(data = loc, fill = "grey93", colour = NA) +
    geom_sf(data = cases[!linked, ], colour = "grey55", size = 0.3) +
    geom_sf(data = res$space_time_link, colour = "grey40", linewidth = 0.25, alpha = 0.6) +
    geom_sf(data = cases[linked, ], aes(colour = week), size = 0.8) +
    scale_colour_manual(values = pal, name = "Week") +
    theme_void()
```

The linked cases (in colour) form the chains; the grey points are cases
without a link. With the real data, `transmission_chains_map()` makes this
map interactive directly from the geocoded dataset:

```{r chains-interactive, eval = FALSE}
transmission_chains_map(geocoded_dataset = sim_dengue,
                        cve_edo = "14", locality = "Guadalajara",
                        dengue_cases = "Probable")
```

## 4. The chains by season

```{r seasons}
by_year <- lapply(2019:2024, function(y) {
    d <- subset(sim_dengue, ANO == y)
    d <- data.frame(x = d$long, y = d$lat, onset = d$FEC_INI_SIGNOS_SINT)
    k <- knox_test(x = d, crs = "+proj=eqc", dt = 20, ds = 400, sym = 999,
                   sp_link = FALSE, planar_coord = FALSE)$knox
    data.frame(year = y, cases = nrow(d), links = k$knox,
               RR = round(k$RR, 2), p_value = k$p_value)
})
do.call(rbind, by_year)
```

## References

- Knox EG (1964). The detection of space-time interactions. *Journal of the
  Royal Statistical Society, Series C*, 13(1), 25-30.
- Tango T (2010). *Statistical Methods for Disease Clustering*. Springer.
